            <ArticleTitle>Clinical Pearl: The &quot;Rule of 8&quot;.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e75-e76</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3928/01913913-20170907-08</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Preschool-aged children typically perform worse than 20/20 on visual acuity testing in the absence of ocular or visual pathway abnormalities due to non-ocular issues, including concentration, cooperation, and/or confidence. As a result, vision screening for this age group can be a challenge. The &quot;Rule of 8&quot; is an easy-to-remember, highly effective mnemonic that clinicians can use to help differentiate children who are performing visual acuity testing at age-appropriate levels from those who may need further evaluation. [J Pediatric Ophthalmol Strabismus. 2017;54:e75-e76.].</AbstractText>
